-- New-style daily words for January (days 1–31): a real devotion (paragraphs ending "Today: …"), a declaration and a prayer. Same KJV verse as before.
-- Only replaces days still in the old one-paragraph style, so anything rewritten in Admin → Devotions is kept.

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'When God spoke these words through Isaiah, His people were exiles in Babylon. Just before this verse, He reminds them of the greatest thing He ever did for them: the way through the Red Sea. Then, surprisingly, He says: do not dwell on that. Something new is coming.

God was not telling them to forget His faithfulness. He was telling them not to live in the past, whether the past was full of wonders or full of failures. The God who parted the sea was about to do something they had never seen.

A new year can feel heavy with last year''s disappointments: the business that struggled, the exam that went badly, the prayer that seemed unanswered. Or it can feel like nothing will ever match a past season when God felt close. Either way, God says: look forward. "Behold, I will do a new thing; now it shall spring forth."

He makes a way in the wilderness and rivers in the desert. Deserts do not produce rivers. Only God does.

Today: write down one thing from last year you need to release to God, and one new thing you are trusting Him for this year. Pray over both.',
  declaration = 'God is doing a new thing in my life. I will not live in the past. He is making a way in my wilderness.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for everything You carried me through last year. I release to You the disappointments, the failures and even the good things I keep looking back on. Open my eyes to see the new thing You are doing. Make a way where I see none. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy001' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Lamentations is one of the saddest books in the Bible. It was written after Jerusalem fell: the city burned, the temple destroyed, the people taken away. The writer weeps for chapters. And then, right in the middle of the darkness, he says the most hopeful words: "It is of the LORD''s mercies that we are not consumed... They are new every morning."

He did not wait for the ruins to be rebuilt before he praised God. He found mercy while still sitting in the ashes.

You may be starting this year with something unresolved: a debt, a sickness, a broken relationship, a grief. God''s mercy does not wait until everything is fixed. It meets you this morning, as you are.

Think about how manna worked in the wilderness. God gave enough for each day, and the next morning there was fresh manna again. You could not store up yesterday''s. In the same way, you do not need to live on yesterday''s grace. There is fresh mercy for today, and there will be fresh mercy tomorrow.

Today: before you check your phone or messages, say aloud: "Great is Your faithfulness, Lord." Then name one mercy you can see this morning.',
  declaration = 'God''s mercies are new for me this morning. His compassion has not run out. Great is His faithfulness.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You that Your mercy did not run out while I slept. Even in the things that still hurt, I choose to see Your faithfulness. Give me fresh grace for today, and help me trust You for tomorrow''s mercy when tomorrow comes. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy002' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Some people in Corinth judged others by outward things: background, status, how impressive they looked. Paul says he used to see even Jesus that way, but not anymore. Everything changes when someone is "in Christ". They are not just a better version of their old self. They are a new creation.

That word "creation" matters. It is the language of Genesis, of God making something from nothing. What God does in a believer is not a repair job or a fresh coat of paint on an old building. It is a new beginning, from the inside.

Many of us still carry old labels: "the one who failed", "the difficult child", "the one with that past", "the one who never finishes anything". People may still use those names. You may still use them on yourself. But God does not. In Christ, the old has passed away.

This does not mean you will never struggle again. It means your struggles no longer define who you are. You are learning to live as the new person God already says you are.

Today: write down one old label you have believed about yourself. Cross it out, and next to it write: "In Christ, I am a new creation." Read it again tonight.',
  declaration = 'I am in Christ, so I am a new creation. The old has passed away; everything has become new.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for making me new in Jesus. I refuse to live under the old labels, the old shame and the old record. Teach me to see myself the way You see me, and to live like the new person You have made me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy003' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Jesus said these words after talking about worry. Food, drink, clothes, tomorrow: He knew what keeps people awake at night. He pointed to the birds that do not farm yet are fed, and to the flowers that do not sew yet are beautifully dressed. Then He gave the key: "seek ye first the kingdom of God."

He did not say our needs are unimportant. He said God already knows we need them. The issue is order. When God is first, everything else finds its proper place.

It is like buttoning a shirt. If the first button goes into the right hole, the rest line up. If the first is wrong, every other button is wrong too, no matter how carefully you work. Many of our plans feel crooked because the first button is out of place.

Seeking His kingdom first is practical. It looks like giving Him the first part of your day, asking what pleases Him before you decide, being honest in business when it costs you, and serving people when no one is watching.

Today: look at your plans for this week. Pick one thing you will do first, before the rest, as a way of putting God first. Then do it.',
  declaration = 'I seek God''s kingdom first. My Father knows what I need, and He will add everything I need to me.',
  prayer = 'Father, forgive me for the times I have put You after my plans and worries. Today I choose to seek You first. Take the first place in my time, my decisions and my desires, and help me trust You to provide the rest. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy004' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Habakkuk had complained to God about the injustice he saw everywhere, and then he climbed his watchtower to wait for an answer. God answered: write the vision down, make it plain, so that whoever reads it can run with it. Then came the hard part: "though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come."

God''s plans have an appointed time. Not our time, His. Sometimes the waiting is the longest part of the promise.

Writing things down is a simple act of faith. Joseph had dreams for years before he saw them fulfilled. Many of us have things God has placed on our hearts: a ministry, a business, a family restored, a nation changed. But they stay vague, so we forget them or give up on them.

When you write it down, you are saying: "I believe God put this here, and I will hold on to it." When it seems slow, you can go back to what you wrote and remind yourself of what God said, not what your feelings say.

Today: take ten minutes to write down what you sense God is calling you towards this year. Keep it simple and plain. Pray over it, and put it where you will see it again.',
  declaration = 'The vision God gave me will surely come at its appointed time. I will write it down and wait for it in faith.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for the things You have placed in my heart. Help me write them down clearly and hold them in faith. When the waiting is long, keep me patient and expectant. Bring Your vision to pass in Your time. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy005' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 32 begins with David''s relief after he finally confessed his sin. He had kept quiet for a long time, and it wore him out. When he opened up to God, he found forgiveness. Then God answers him with this promise: "I will instruct thee and teach thee in the way which thou shalt go."

The order is important. Guidance follows honesty. God is glad to guide a heart that has stopped hiding from Him.

Notice too how personal it is: "I will guide thee with mine eye." It is the picture of someone so close that a look is enough. Think of a mother at a family party who only needs to glance at her child across the room, and the child understands. That kind of guidance only works when you are close enough to see the face.

The next verse warns us not to be like a horse or a mule that has to be pulled with a bit and bridle. God does not want to drag us. He wants us near enough to follow His look.

Today: before you make one decision today, big or small, pause and ask, "Lord, what would please You here?" Then wait a moment before you decide.',
  declaration = 'God instructs me and teaches me the way I should go. I stay close enough to follow His guidance.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for forgiving me and for wanting to guide me. I do not want to be pulled like a stubborn horse. Keep me close to You, honest before You and quick to follow when You show me the way. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy006' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Judah was in trouble, and instead of turning to God, they sent messengers to Egypt for help. Isaiah warned them that this would only bring shame. Yet even then, God promised that a day would come when His people would hear His voice behind them, saying, "This is the way, walk ye in it."

It is a picture of a shepherd walking behind the flock, calling out when a sheep starts to drift to the left or the right. You are not walking alone, and you are not expected to know every turn in advance.

We often want God to show us the whole map: the job, the city, the spouse, the next five years. More often He gives us a voice for the next step. And we hear it best when we stop running to our own "Egypt", the quick solutions we trust more than Him.

How does God speak? Most often through His Word, through the quiet nudge of His Spirit in prayer, through wise and godly counsel, and through open and closed doors. He will never lead you against what Scripture says.

Today: think of a decision you are facing. Write down what you sense God is saying through His Word, through prayer and through wise people. Then take one small, obedient step.',
  declaration = 'I hear the voice of my Shepherd. When I turn to the right or the left, He shows me the way to walk.',
  prayer = 'Lord, forgive me for the times I have run to my own solutions instead of You. Tune my ears to Your voice. When I begin to drift, call me back, and give me the courage to walk in the way You show me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy007' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The Hebrew word translated "commit" in this verse means to roll something onto someone else. Picture a market woman rolling a heavy sack off her head onto a strong cart. The weight is still real, but she is no longer carrying it alone.

That is what God invites us to do with our work, our plans, our business, our studies. Roll them onto Him. And the promise is that our thoughts, our plans, will be established: made firm, steady and settled.

This is not a promise that God will rubber-stamp whatever we decide. The chapter begins by saying that people make their plans, but the final answer comes from the Lord. Committing our work to Him means we invite Him to adjust it, redirect it or even stop it.

When we do, something changes inside us. Anxious plans become peaceful plans. We still work hard, we still think carefully, but we are no longer trying to hold up the whole world on our own heads.

Today: name the main piece of work in front of you this week: a project, an exam, a sale, a difficult task. Before you start it, say out loud: "Lord, I roll this onto You. Establish it Your way."',
  declaration = 'I commit my work to the Lord. He establishes my plans and gives me peace in my labour.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I roll my work, my plans and my worries onto You. Correct what is wrong, strengthen what is right and lead me in the way that pleases You. Give me peace as I work, knowing You are carrying this with me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy008' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote this letter in chains. Just before this verse, he listed everything he could boast about: his family line, his education, his religious zeal. Then he said he counts it all as loss compared with knowing Christ. And he adds, "I count not myself to have apprehended." Even Paul had not arrived.

What did he do instead? "This one thing I do": forget what lies behind, reach for what is ahead, and press toward the prize.

Forgetting does not mean losing your memory. It means refusing to let the past hold you back. That includes past failures that make you feel unworthy, and past successes that make you comfortable.

Picture a relay runner who keeps looking back over her shoulder. She will lose speed, and she may even fall. Runners look forward. The track is ahead.

Maybe you are carrying a past mistake you keep replaying, or a past achievement you keep resting on. Paul''s word to you is the same: press on. The prize is still ahead, and the One who called you has not stopped calling.

Today: write down one thing from your past you keep looking back at. Pray over it, then tear up the paper as a sign that you are pressing forward.',
  declaration = 'I forget what is behind and reach for what is ahead. I press toward the prize of God''s high calling in Christ Jesus.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, I do not want to be held back by my past, neither my failures nor my successes. Help me release what is behind me. Fix my eyes on You and on the calling You have given me, and give me strength to keep pressing on. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy009' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 90 is the only psalm credited to Moses. He had seen a whole generation pass away in the wilderness. He knew how short life is: "we spend our years as a tale that is told." So he prayed, "teach us to number our days."

Numbering our days is not about fear. It is about wisdom. When we remember that our days are limited, we stop wasting them on things that do not matter, and we start investing them in what lasts.

Most of us count money carefully. We know what is in our account, what is owed, what is coming. But few of us count our days with the same care. Hours disappear into worry, scrolling, quarrels and grudges that will not matter in a year.

God is not asking you to be busy every minute. Rest is also a wise use of time. He is asking you to live on purpose: to love people while you can, to forgive quickly, to say what needs saying, to seek Him while He may be found.

Today: at the end of the day, ask yourself three questions. What did I do today that will last? What did I waste time on? What will I do differently tomorrow? Then thank God for the gift of the day.',
  declaration = 'My days are a gift from God. I will number them wisely and live this day on purpose.',
  prayer = 'Lord, teach me to number my days. Forgive me for the hours I have wasted on things that do not matter. Give me a heart of wisdom, so that I spend my time on what pleases You and blesses the people You have placed around me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy010' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Hebrews 11 is sometimes called the hall of faith. It lists people like Abel, Noah, Abraham, Sarah and Moses. But it starts with a definition: faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.

The word translated "substance" can mean a title deed: the document that proves something is yours even before you move in. Faith is like holding the deed to a house you have not yet seen with your eyes. You cannot see the house, but you have the papers.

Many of the people in this chapter died without seeing everything God promised. Abraham was promised a land and a nation, but he lived in tents. Yet they did not stop believing, because their faith was not in what they could see. It was in the God who promised.

This is the faith that pleases God: not a feeling, not positive thinking, but trust in His character and His word. It keeps walking when the road is dark. It keeps praying when the answer is slow.

Today: choose one promise of God from Scripture that speaks to your situation. Write it down and carry it with you. Every time you worry, read it again, as your title deed.',
  declaration = 'My faith rests on God''s word, not on what I can see. What He has promised is mine.',
  prayer = 'Father, I believe You. Even when I cannot see what You are doing, I trust Your character and Your promises. Strengthen my faith, and help me keep walking in it when the road is long. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy011' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'This psalm was written to people who were troubled by how well evil people seemed to be doing. David''s answer was not to compete with them or to worry about them, but to delight in the Lord.

"Delight thyself also in the LORD: and he shall give thee the desires of thine heart." Some read this as a formula: enjoy God, and He will give you whatever you want. But something deeper happens. As you delight in Him, your desires begin to change. You start wanting what He wants. And those desires He is glad to fulfil.

Then comes verse 5: "Commit thy way unto the LORD; trust also in him; and he shall bring it to pass." The word "commit" here also means to roll, like rolling a heavy load onto someone stronger.

Delight is about the heart. Commitment is about the will. Trust is about patience. Together they describe a life that is relaxed in God''s hands, even when others seem to be getting ahead faster.

Today: spend five minutes simply enjoying God, not asking for anything. Thank Him for who He is, sing a song you love, or read a psalm slowly. Then roll your plans onto Him and leave them there.',
  declaration = 'I delight in the Lord. I commit my way to Him and trust Him, and He brings it to pass.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I want to delight in You, not only in what You give. Shape my desires until they match Yours. I roll my way onto You today. Help me trust You and wait patiently while You bring Your good plans to pass. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy012' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David wrote this psalm as a morning prayer. He says two things: "in the morning will I direct my prayer unto thee", and "will look up".

The first is about preparation. The word translated "direct" was used for arranging wood on an altar or setting out a case before a judge. David was not just mumbling a quick prayer. He was laying his day out before God in order.

The second is about expectation. After praying, he watched, like a watchman on the city wall looking for the first light of dawn, or like someone who has sent a message and keeps checking for the reply.

Many of us pray in the morning, then forget we prayed. We do not expect anything. But David expected God to answer, to act, to guide. That expectation changed how he walked through the rest of his day.

Your morning sets the tone for the whole day. You do not need to pray for an hour. But give God your first words, before the noise starts, and then live the day looking for His answers.

Today: before you leave your room, lay out your day before God: your tasks, your worries, the people you will meet. Then, through the day, look for one sign that He heard you.',
  declaration = 'In the morning God hears my voice. I lay my day before Him and I look up, expecting Him to answer.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I give You my first words today. Here is my day: my work, my family, my worries and my hopes. I lay them before You in order. Help me walk through this day looking up, expecting You to act. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy013' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'God spoke these words through Ezekiel to His people in exile. They had broken His laws again and again, and they were scattered among the nations because of it. God did not say, "Try harder this time." He said, "I will give you a new heart."

A stony heart is a heart that has become hard: hard toward God, hard toward people, maybe hard from too much pain. Stone does not feel. It does not soften. You cannot squeeze life out of it.

Some hearts become stone through sin. Others become stone through hurt: betrayal, loss, disappointment, a prayer that seemed to go unheard. People protect themselves by not feeling anymore.

God''s promise is not just to forgive, but to transform. He takes out the stone and puts in a heart of flesh: a heart that can feel, love, repent, trust and respond to Him again. And He adds His own Spirit, so we are not trying to live this new life in our own strength.

No heart is too hard for God. He is not just a teacher who tells you what to do. He is a Saviour who changes who you are.

Today: ask God honestly, "Where has my heart become hard?" Name it, and invite Him to soften that place.',
  declaration = 'God has given me a new heart and a new spirit. Where I was hard, He makes me soft, alive and responsive to Him.',
  prayer = 'Father, I bring You the hard places in my heart: the hurts, the bitterness, the coldness toward You and toward people. Take away the stone. Give me a heart of flesh, and fill me with Your Spirit, so I can love You and others again. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy014' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 119 is the longest chapter in the Bible, and almost every verse is about God''s Word. In the middle of it is this picture: "Thy word is a lamp unto my feet, and a light unto my path."

In those days, a lamp was a small clay bowl with oil and a wick. It did not light up the whole road. It gave enough light for the next few steps. You would hold it low, near your feet, so you would not stumble on stones or fall into a hole.

That is often how God guides through His Word. We want floodlights showing the whole journey: where we will live, who we will marry, what will happen to our business. God often gives a lamp instead: enough light for the next step.

If you have ever walked home during a power cut with only your phone torch, you know how it works. You cannot see the end of the street. But you can see the next step, and that is enough to get home safely, one step at a time.

The Word does not just give information. It gives light. It shows where the stones and holes are, and where the safe ground is.

Today: read a short passage of Scripture slowly. Ask God, "What is the next step You are showing me?" Then take it.',
  declaration = 'God''s Word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path. I will take the next step He shows me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for Your Word. When I cannot see the whole road, let it light my next step. Keep me from stumbling, and give me patience to walk by the light You give, one step at a time. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy015' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah 58 is about the kind of fasting God wants: not empty religion, but feeding the hungry, freeing the oppressed and caring for the poor. And to people who live like that, God makes beautiful promises. This is one of them.

"The LORD shall guide thee continually, and satisfy thy soul in drought... and thou shalt be like a watered garden, and like a spring of water, whose waters fail not."

Think of the difference between a bucket and a spring. A bucket can be full, but every time you pour from it, it gets emptier. A spring keeps flowing because it is connected to a source deep underground.

Many of us live like buckets. We give at work, at home, in church, to family, until we are empty and dry. God wants to make us springs: people whose life keeps flowing because we are connected to Him.

Notice too where this promise is given: in drought. The watered garden is green even when everything around it is brown. That is not because the drought is not real. It is because the garden has a hidden source.

Today: ask yourself where you are feeling dry. Spend unhurried time with God today, not to work for Him, but to drink from Him.',
  declaration = 'The Lord guides me continually and satisfies my soul in drought. I am like a watered garden and a spring that never fails.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I confess I have been running on empty. Guide me continually. Satisfy my soul even in this dry season. Make my life like a watered garden, and let what flows from me come from You, not just from my own strength. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy016' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'James wrote to believers going through many kinds of trials. He told them that trials produce patience, and patience makes us complete. Then, almost immediately, he says: "If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask of God."

That is exactly when we need wisdom most: in the middle of a problem, when we do not know what to do.

Look at how God gives: "liberally, and upbraideth not." Liberally means generously, with an open hand. "Upbraideth not" means He does not scold you for asking. He will not say, "You should have known this already," or "Why are you asking me again?"

Some of us were raised to feel foolish for asking questions. We would rather guess than admit we do not know. But God is not like an impatient teacher. He is a generous Father who wants His children to grow wise.

Wisdom is more than knowledge. Knowledge knows the facts. Wisdom knows what to do with them: how to speak to that difficult relative, how to handle that money decision, when to wait and when to act.

James adds one condition: ask in faith, without doubting. Ask expecting God to answer.

Today: name the situation where you most need wisdom right now. Ask God for it plainly, and expect Him to give it through His Word, His Spirit or wise counsel.',
  declaration = 'When I lack wisdom, I ask God, and He gives it to me generously without scolding me.',
  prayer = 'Father, I need wisdom for the things in front of me. I do not know what to do in every situation, but You do. Give me Your wisdom generously, as You promised. Help me ask in faith and recognise Your answer when it comes. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy017' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'This psalm is full of confidence. David says God is his portion, his inheritance, and that because the Lord is at his right hand he will not be moved. On the day of Pentecost, Peter quoted this same psalm and said it pointed to the resurrection of Jesus.

Then comes this beautiful verse: "Thou wilt shew me the path of life: in thy presence is fulness of joy; at thy right hand there are pleasures for evermore."

The world offers many paths that promise joy: success, money, popularity, pleasure. Some give a little happiness for a while. But they cannot give fullness of joy, joy that is complete, that does not leak away.

God does not only show us the path. He is the joy on the path. The fullness of joy is not in a place or a possession, but in His presence.

This is why someone can have very little and still be deeply joyful, and someone can have everything and still feel empty. Joy is not about what you have. It is about whom you are with.

Today: find a quiet moment to simply be in God''s presence. Tell Him, "You are my joy." Notice whether anything you are chasing is actually a search for what only He can give.',
  declaration = 'God shows me the path of life. In His presence is fullness of joy, and at His right hand are pleasures for evermore.',
  prayer = 'Lord, show me the path of life. Forgive me for looking for joy in places that cannot give it. Draw me into Your presence, and let my joy be full in You, whatever I have or do not have today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy018' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In the Sermon on the Mount, Jesus says: "Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you."

In the original Greek, these verbs suggest continuing action: keep on asking, keep on seeking, keep on knocking. Jesus is not describing a single prayer, but a persistent way of coming to God.

And notice the progression. Asking is speaking. Seeking adds effort: looking, searching, moving. Knocking adds persistence at a closed door.

Then Jesus explains why we can pray like this. If a father would not give his son a stone when he asks for bread, or a snake when he asks for fish, how much more will our heavenly Father give good things to those who ask Him?

Prayer is not bothering God. He is not a busy office that wants you to stop calling. He is a Father who wants to hear from His children.

Sometimes the door opens quickly. Sometimes it takes longer. Sometimes He gives something better than what we asked for. But He always hears, and He always gives good things.

Today: is there something you stopped praying about because nothing seemed to happen? Bring it to God again today. Ask, seek, knock, and keep doing it.',
  declaration = 'I keep asking, keep seeking and keep knocking. My Father hears me and gives good things to those who ask.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that You are not tired of hearing from me. Here are the things I have stopped praying about. I bring them to You again. Give me persistence in prayer, and trust that You will give what is truly good. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy019' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David wrote this psalm while surrounded by enemies. Armies were camped against him, false witnesses were rising up, and he felt the pressure from every side. Yet in the middle of all that, his deepest desire was not escape or victory. It was this: "One thing have I desired of the LORD... that I may dwell in the house of the LORD all the days of my life, to behold the beauty of the LORD."

David had many desires: safety, success, his throne secured. But one desire rose above all the others: to be near God and to see His beauty.

Most of us have a long list of desires for this year: a better job, a healed body, a peaceful home, a breakthrough. These are not wrong. But when our list is long and our love for God is short, we lose our way.

Mary of Bethany understood this. While her sister Martha was busy and worried about many things, Mary sat at Jesus'' feet. Jesus said, "one thing is needful: and Mary hath chosen that good part."

When God is our one thing, everything else finds its place.

Today: write down your list of desires for this year. Then, at the top, write: "One thing: to know You, Lord." Pray through your list with that as your first desire.',
  declaration = 'One thing I desire above all: to dwell in God''s presence and behold His beauty all the days of my life.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I have many desires, but let You be my one thing. Draw me to Your presence. Let me see Your beauty, even in difficult seasons. And let every other desire in my heart find its proper place under my love for You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy020' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'God speaks here of leading people who cannot see, along roads they have never walked before. The promise is full of tenderness: "I will make darkness light before them, and crooked things straight. These things will I do unto them, and not forsake them."

Maybe you are walking an unfamiliar road this year: a new city, a new job, a new marriage, life as a widow or widower, an illness you did not expect, a business you have never run before. You feel blind, because you have never been this way.

God does not promise to show you the whole road in advance. He promises to lead you. A blind person walking with a trusted guide does not need to see the potholes. He only needs to hold the guide''s arm.

Notice also the promise about crooked things. On the road, God goes ahead and straightens things you could never straighten yourself.

And the last line is the strongest: "and not forsake them." He does not lead you halfway and leave you.

Today: name the unfamiliar road you are walking. Tell God honestly what you cannot see. Then hold on to this promise: He will lead you, make darkness light and not forsake you.',
  declaration = 'God leads me on paths I have not known. He makes darkness light before me and crooked things straight, and He will not forsake me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I am walking a road I have never walked before. I cannot see far ahead, but You can. Lead me. Turn my darkness into light, straighten what is crooked, and keep me close. Thank You that You will not forsake me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy021' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'David prayed this psalm in great distress. His enemy was pursuing him, his spirit was overwhelmed, and he felt like someone sitting in darkness. Yet his prayer is beautiful: "Cause me to hear thy lovingkindness in the morning; for in thee do I trust."

Before he asked for direction, he asked to hear God''s love. That is the right order. When you are afraid, the first thing you need is not a plan. It is the assurance that you are loved.

"Lovingkindness" translates a rich Hebrew word, hesed. It means God''s loyal, covenant love: love that keeps its promises and does not give up.

Then comes the next request: "cause me to know the way wherein I should walk; for I lift up my soul unto thee." Lifting up your soul is like a child lifting up her arms to her father, asking to be carried.

Many of us start the day with news, messages and worries. The first voice we hear shapes the rest of the day. David chose to make God''s love the first voice.

Today: before you check your phone tomorrow morning, pray this verse slowly. Ask God to let His love be the first thing you hear, and then ask Him to show you the way.',
  declaration = 'In the morning I hear God''s lovingkindness, and I trust Him. I lift up my soul to Him, and He shows me the way to walk.',
  prayer = 'Lord, let Your love be the first thing I hear each morning. In the middle of my worries, remind me that You are faithful and You have not let me go. I lift up my soul to You. Show me the way I should walk today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy022' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Moses was 120 years old and about to die. He would not cross the Jordan with the people he had led for forty years. Joshua would take them into the promised land, where there were walled cities and strong enemies. So Moses gathered them and said: "the LORD, he it is that doth go before thee; he will be with thee, he will not fail thee, neither forsake thee: fear not, neither be dismayed."

Notice the two promises: God goes before you, and God goes with you. He is already in your tomorrow, preparing the way, and He is also beside you today.

Leaders change. Moses would not be with them anymore. Parents grow old, mentors move on, friends relocate, pastors are transferred. That can make us feel unprotected. But the One who really led Israel was never Moses. It was God, and He does not die or move away.

Joshua would face Jericho, a city with great walls. But God had already gone ahead of him there.

Today: think of something ahead of you that makes you anxious: a meeting, a journey, a hospital visit, an exam, a new season. Picture God already standing there, waiting for you. Then step forward without fear.',
  declaration = 'The Lord goes before me and is with me. He will not fail me or forsake me. I will not fear or be dismayed.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You that You are already in my tomorrow. Go before me into every situation I am afraid of. Walk beside me today. Remove my fear, and help me step forward knowing You will not fail me or leave me. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy023' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Proverbs 4, a father is teaching his son. He describes two paths: the path of the wicked, which is "as darkness" so they do not even know what makes them stumble, and the path of the just, which is "as the shining light, that shineth more and more unto the perfect day."

The picture is a sunrise. At 5am it is still mostly dark. By 6am there is a soft grey light. By 7am you can see clearly. By noon the sun is at full strength. The light does not jump from dark to noon in one moment. It grows.

Spiritual growth is often like that. We want instant maturity: to stop that habit immediately, to understand the whole Bible in a month, to be strong in faith tomorrow. But usually God grows us gradually, a little more light each day.

If you compare yourself with where you want to be, you may feel discouraged. But compare yourself with where you were a year ago. Is there more light? More patience? More love? Then the path is shining more and more.

Today: thank God for one specific way you have grown in the last year. Then ask Him for more light in one area where you still feel in the dark.',
  declaration = 'My path is like the shining light that grows brighter and brighter. God is growing me day by day.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for the light You have already brought into my life. Forgive me when I am impatient with myself. Keep growing me, a little more each day, until my life shines with Your light. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy024' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah 26 is a song for a future day of salvation, when God''s people would sing about a strong city protected by Him. In that song comes this promise: "Thou wilt keep him in perfect peace, whose mind is stayed on thee: because he trusteth in thee."

In Hebrew, "perfect peace" is literally "shalom shalom", peace repeated for emphasis. Not just a little calm, but peace upon peace.

And the key is the mind. A mind "stayed" on God is a mind that leans on Him, like a tired person leaning on a strong wall. Our minds lean on something all the time. Often they lean on worries: replaying conversations, imagining what could go wrong, running the numbers again at midnight.

God does not promise to remove every problem. He promises to keep your heart in peace while your mind rests on Him.

How do we keep our minds on God? Through Scripture we memorise and repeat. Through worship songs that fill the quiet. Through short prayers in the middle of the day. Through choosing, again and again, to bring our thoughts back to Him.

Today: choose one short line from Scripture, perhaps this verse. Each time your mind starts to race today, stop and repeat it slowly, then breathe and trust.',
  declaration = 'God keeps me in perfect peace because my mind is stayed on Him and I trust in Him.',
  prayer = 'Lord, my mind runs in so many directions. Teach me to lean my thoughts on You. When worry rises, bring me back to Your Word and Your faithfulness. Keep me in Your perfect peace today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy025' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Micah lived in a dark time. Leaders were corrupt, families were divided and people could not trust even their neighbours. In chapter 7 the prophet speaks for God''s people, and he turns to the enemy who is laughing at their fall: "Rejoice not against me, O mine enemy: when I fall, I shall arise."

He does not pretend there was no fall. He admits it, and even admits that it came because of sin. But he refuses to believe that the fall is the end.

Everyone falls. A failed marriage, a business collapse, a moral failure, a relapse into an old habit, a season of backsliding. The enemy loves to stand over us and say, "You are finished. Look at you."

But God''s people answer: "When I fall, I shall arise; when I sit in darkness, the LORD shall be a light unto me." Peter denied Jesus three times, and Jesus restored him. David sinned terribly, and God forgave and restored him. Our falls are real, but they are not final.

Rising begins with turning toward the light, toward God, honestly.

Today: if you are sitting in the dark after a fall, do not stay there. Tell God the truth, receive His forgiveness, and take one small step toward getting up.',
  declaration = 'When I fall, I shall arise. When I sit in darkness, the Lord is my light.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You know where I have fallen. I will not hide it from You. Forgive me and lift me up. Be my light in this darkness. Silence the voice that says I am finished, and help me rise and walk with You again. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy026' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The title of this psalm tells us that David wrote it "when he was in the wilderness of Judah". He was a fugitive, hiding in dry, rocky land, far from the comforts of the palace and the worship of the tabernacle. And there he wrote: "my soul thirsteth for thee, my flesh longeth for thee in a dry and thirsty land, where no water is."

David felt the dryness of the land, and he recognised a deeper dryness inside. But he did not run from it. He let it turn him toward God.

We often misread our inner thirst. We feel restless and empty, and we try to fill it with food, entertainment, scrolling, shopping, relationships or busyness. But the deepest thirst in every human heart is for God Himself.

"Early will I seek thee" can also mean "earnestly will I seek thee". Seeking God early in the day is a beautiful habit, but the heart of it is earnestness: wanting Him, not just doing a religious duty.

Later in the psalm, David says, "my soul shall be satisfied as with marrow and fatness." Seeking God in the desert leads to satisfaction.

Today: when you feel restless or empty, pause and ask, "Am I really thirsty for God?" Then take a few minutes to seek Him earnestly instead of reaching for a distraction.',
  declaration = 'O God, You are my God. I seek You earnestly, and in dry places my soul is satisfied in You.',
  prayer = 'O God, You are my God. I am thirsty for You in this dry land. Forgive me for trying to satisfy my soul with things that cannot fill it. Meet me in my dryness, and satisfy me with Your presence. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy027' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Many of us know this verse as a cheerful song. But in Psalm 118, it comes right after a powerful statement: "The stone which the builders refused is become the head stone of the corner. This is the LORD''s doing; it is marvellous in our eyes."

The "day" the psalmist is celebrating is the day God turned rejection into victory. Jesus later quoted these verses about Himself. He was the stone the builders rejected, and God made Him the cornerstone through His death and resurrection.

So "This is the day which the LORD hath made" is not just about having a good mood. It is about seeing God''s hand in your day, even in what looked like rejection or defeat.

Today might not feel special. It might be ordinary or even hard. But it is a day the Lord made, and He is at work in it. You may not see the full picture yet, just as the disciples did not understand the cross on the day Jesus died.

Choosing to rejoice is not pretending everything is fine. It is trusting that God is doing something marvellous, even when we cannot yet see it.

Today: name three things you can thank God for today, including one thing that looked like a setback. Then say it aloud: "This is the day the Lord has made."',
  declaration = 'This is the day the Lord has made. He is at work in it, and I will rejoice and be glad in it.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for this day. Even where I see setbacks, help me trust that You are working. Turn what others reject into something marvellous for Your glory. Give me a glad heart to rejoice in You today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy028' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Just before this verse, the writer of Hebrews mentions Enoch, who walked with God and "pleased God". Then he explains what made that possible: "without faith it is impossible to please him."

God is not pleased by our perfection, our religious activity or our impressive prayers. He is pleased by trust.

The verse gives two parts of this faith. First, believe "that he is": that God is real, present and personal, not just an idea. Second, believe that "he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him": that seeking Him is never wasted.

That second part matters. Many people believe God exists, but deep down they doubt He cares, or that time with Him makes any difference. They pray, but without expectation.

The reward of seeking God is not always money or success. Often the greatest reward is God Himself: His presence, His peace, His guidance and a deeper friendship with Him. But He does reward, and He sees every prayer prayed in secret.

"Diligently" means seeking with care and effort, not casually. Enoch walked with God for hundreds of years. Faith is not one leap, but a daily walk.

Today: seek God diligently for a few minutes today, believing He is near and that this time matters to Him. Tell Him, "I believe You are, and I believe You reward those who seek You."',
  declaration = 'I believe that God is, and that He rewards those who diligently seek Him. My faith pleases Him.',
  prayer = 'Father, I believe You are real and near. I believe You reward those who seek You. Help me seek You diligently, not casually. Grow my faith, so my life pleases You like Enoch''s did. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy029' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Psalm 25 is a prayer of someone who feels the weight of his sins, his loneliness and his troubles. But it is also a learner''s prayer: "Shew me thy ways, O LORD; teach me thy paths. Lead me in thy truth, and teach me."

There are three requests: show me, teach me, lead me. Showing is like pointing at the road. Teaching is explaining how to walk it. Leading is walking with someone, step by step.

The psalmist does not ask God to give him a list of rules and leave. He asks God to be his teacher and guide. Later in the psalm he says God teaches sinners in the way, and guides the meek in judgment. God is patient with learners, even those who have made mistakes.

Then he says, "on thee do I wait all the day." Learning from God often involves waiting. A good student does not rush the teacher. A good traveller does not run ahead of the guide.

We live in a world of instant answers. We search online and expect results in seconds. But God''s way of teaching is relational and patient. He shapes us over time.

Today: ask God to teach you one thing this week. Read a short passage of Scripture each day with this prayer: "Show me, teach me, lead me." Write down what you learn.',
  declaration = 'The Lord shows me His ways, teaches me His paths and leads me in His truth. I wait on Him all day.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I want to learn from You. Show me Your ways, teach me Your paths and lead me in Your truth. Be patient with me as I learn, and help me be patient as I wait on You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy030' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'This psalm is David''s advice from a long life. He says, "I have been young, and now am old; yet have I not seen the righteous forsaken." And in the middle of the psalm comes this encouragement: "The steps of a good man are ordered by the LORD: and he delighteth in his way."

God does not only care about where you will end up. He cares about each step: the conversations you have, the turns you take, the doors that open and close.

And He delights in your way. He is not watching you with irritation, waiting for you to fail. He takes pleasure in walking with you.

Then comes the comfort: "Though he fall, he shall not be utterly cast down: for the LORD upholdeth him with his hand." Notice that it does not say a good person will never fall. It says the fall will not be total. God''s hand is underneath.

Think of a father teaching his child to walk. The child stumbles many times. But the father''s hand is always close, and the child is never allowed to crash completely.

As January ends, you may look back and see stumbles alongside progress. God has been holding your hand through it all.

Today: thank God for one step He ordered this month, and for one time He caught you when you stumbled.',
  declaration = 'The Lord orders my steps and delights in my way. Even if I stumble, I will not be cast down, because He holds my hand.',
  prayer = 'Lord, thank You for ordering my steps this month. Thank You for the times You caught me when I stumbled. Keep holding my hand. Order my steps in the month ahead, and let my way bring You delight. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy031' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;
